#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124286 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/id #uuid "2230e2dd-e47a-5db3-bb07-f89d0396440b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6573" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(t) = e^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 3y ' - 4y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(t)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2} = u(t)e^{-t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t})-3({u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t})-4u{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-5{u}^{′}{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-t}({u}^{″}-5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^t \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' - 5u' = 0.\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' - 5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 e^{5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, since since $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1e^{5t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(t) = u e^{-t}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t}\\left( \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = 5$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t} \\cdot e^{5t} = e^{4t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124287 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bd03341b-1190-51fa-b3f0-6fd921e3430c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6574" :question/problem "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0.$ If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$\n\n- $u''=0$\n\n- $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n\n- $u''-u=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x){e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x})-6({u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x})+9u{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}({u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}+9u-6{u}^{′}-18u+9u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{3x} \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27bff131-1e20-5aaa-87d6-56abf25934fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-8{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342511} {:answer/id #uuid "cd17fe2e-54cd-5afd-ae1f-ec24b6b1d796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}-2u=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342512} {:answer/id #uuid "92430325-68ed-5de2-8547-5785ecf9194b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342513} {:answer/id #uuid "b1de963b-3f55-52da-8196-11499511b9d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+2u=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342514} {:answer/id #uuid "c8588df4-2eb2-507c-98f3-c1063bd26d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8588df4-2eb2-507c-98f3-c1063bd26d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342515} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac83e7f-8531-5999-a8c1-f62b282b1671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0750bb6-59a2-532b-9169-c3620b5de961" :question/math-academy-id "q-102792" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-3t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-3y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{-3t}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}-3u{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-6t}+9u{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-6t}+9u{e}^{-3t})+2({u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}-3u{e}^{-3t})-3u{e}^{-3t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3t}({u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}+9u+2{u}^{′}-6u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3t}({u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49241e1a-112f-520a-a588-e279e39175ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-{t}^{2}{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342518} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd4ca20-a8e9-50c1-b006-bb825ae039c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-2t{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342519} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a9c835-787f-5774-a6db-d9057e828f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{u}^{″}-{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342520} {:answer/id #uuid "fa34d0e7-6b57-51a5-9a63-b6fc84966bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342521} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4f90cd-1e80-528c-95cd-7915578e0d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa34d0e7-6b57-51a5-9a63-b6fc84966bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342521} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd6df1d1-8b18-593a-94b2-27972f62bdb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd891361-e379-54a9-be99-b7079ca7d049" :question/math-academy-id "q-102793" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{-2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-2y=0$ for $t>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{t}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{t}^{-2}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{-2}-2u{t}^{-3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{-3}+6u{t}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{-3}+6u{t}^{-4})+2t({u}^{′}{t}^{-2}-2u{t}^{-3})-2u{t}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{-1}+6u{t}^{-2}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-4u{t}^{-2}-2u{t}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{-1}({u}^{″}t-2{u}^{′}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}({u}^{″}t-2{u}^{′}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{t}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8b20ceb-544b-5873-93d4-481127afbb1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-2(x+1){u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342525} {:answer/id #uuid "cd634bf5-f922-55b5-b2a9-c8796d3a31a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342526} {:answer/id #uuid "d74625a0-c039-5c49-b6e0-a2f5a1c0976c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-4(x+1){u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342527} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8dd46a-0794-541a-bafc-92ae41838bfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{u}^{″}-2(x+1){u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342528} {:answer/id #uuid "a38401bf-81a2-52ff-a209-038769b87bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd634bf5-f922-55b5-b2a9-c8796d3a31a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342526} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fca60a6-2994-5965-98ff-c7667770a3a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b951cde6-d40f-5f34-8249-bb7957fdc2d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-102794" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2x(x+1){y}^{′}+2(x+1)y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=ux.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=ux$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}x+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′})-2x(x+1)({u}^{′}x+u)+2(x+1)(ux) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}{x}^{2}(x+1)-2ux(x+1)+2ux(x+1) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}{x}^{3}-2{u}^{′}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}-2{u}^{′}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f79fb2-b988-5e2d-80a1-756d74d6b89c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342532} {:answer/id #uuid "30455aef-cef7-51dd-9b3b-e6dcb64f84bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342533} {:answer/id #uuid "992e868b-9615-58ec-a175-20f5de5c77c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342534} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf4745c-ca01-56f1-8f88-737f091d06d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342535} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4bf3ca-a658-5445-b84b-d6a2dbf62541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf4745c-ca01-56f1-8f88-737f091d06d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342535} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5deb247-c10b-5866-8ddd-bb67a2f92f89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac813398-e89a-5bcd-88b5-31780b4568a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-102800" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{4x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{4x}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+16u{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{4x}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+16u{e}^{4x})-3({u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x})-4u{e}^{4x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{4x}({u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}+16u-3{u}^{′}-12u-4u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{4x}({u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4x}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-5w.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-5x}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5}{e}^{-5x}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{4x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5}{e}^{-5x}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-5x}={e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc37f426-29c4-5b98-8087-a9487fb96c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342539} {:answer/id #uuid "f35ac8c5-9ee7-5f51-86a1-babf31e904bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342540} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4b18da-48ab-50fd-9a77-f960e051980c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342541} {:answer/id #uuid "b3316355-c80c-5335-adbe-336ae89b80f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342542} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9a8814-d824-5759-9d85-b4121fbd2889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a4b18da-48ab-50fd-9a77-f960e051980c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342541} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe1fa1ac-b829-5bb7-8d4f-481660bf1dcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0b4e05c-493c-513f-8d82-76dddaa02634" :question/math-academy-id "q-64953" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}={e}^{x}({u}^{′}+u) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u)-2{e}^{x}({u}^{′}+u)+{e}^{x}(u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u-2{u}^{′}-2u+u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0\\,⟹\\,w={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot x=x{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c5fc7fc-2f90-556e-a8ba-841ee4345c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123611} {:answer/id #uuid "d996ab88-8a1c-5c7e-bf9f-eab5d48182f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123612} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4f5584-8fc7-5337-ab56-9e4c5e615f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123613} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3ceaf6-e888-5fb4-82cc-4ead9996c36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123614} {:answer/id #uuid "3a307f38-89b4-5782-9efc-cba840fb9d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d996ab88-8a1c-5c7e-bf9f-eab5d48182f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123612} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a5512a1-ab4e-5f23-a650-68685dcdaff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ed6836b6-963c-5315-9c37-229f40227bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-64958" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}-3y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-3u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-{u}^{′}x-3u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-4$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0e87fa0-cbe5-5c8f-a25f-becfe555c70d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xu^{″} - 2u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123623} {:answer/id #uuid "eae3394f-8ba9-59fe-98b7-7cd80dd4b515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xu^{″} + 5u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123624} {:answer/id #uuid "2d798297-8017-5f5d-8095-f835eb411ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xu^{″} + 7u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123625} {:answer/id #uuid "33f2244c-0581-5881-9396-fff7fef1171e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xu^{″} + 3u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123626} {:answer/id #uuid "43008340-987a-5945-b230-c8a5d3713242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xu^{″} - 5u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d798297-8017-5f5d-8095-f835eb411ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xu^{″} + 7u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123625} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f9e258c-0697-50bb-854f-78cc38193521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a18ec5a-2e1b-5a0c-b567-88ee298d1b67" :question/math-academy-id "q-64995" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}-5y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=ux.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=ux$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}x+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′})+5x({u}^{′}x+u)-5ux & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+5ux-5ux & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+7{u}^{′}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+7{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nx{u}^{″}+7{u}^{′}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c49977a9-ac11-5940-8fd1-97d5fb0341de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342546} {:answer/id #uuid "2c75840c-2d94-531b-8d9f-a96f1ae1a23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342547} {:answer/id #uuid "cd102047-2d61-512e-918c-3838a762c61a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342548} {:answer/id #uuid "dbbfab88-5e2c-5129-8fc9-51fe7cbf9b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342549} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe637e3-435a-5a7f-b032-f5a4f17c934a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c75840c-2d94-531b-8d9f-a96f1ae1a23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342547} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47a58ef-ea6d-5f72-bea3-4ba11429fa49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16ff2884-3d19-5ae0-a798-f85100f86ae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64998" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-6w\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e718a58-cfbc-5efe-9414-0ef1ab930662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342553} {:answer/id #uuid "648e0682-b58c-53a8-a699-c7ea9ca86128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342554} {:answer/id #uuid "54918d71-7b8c-52bf-ba39-c9e57a0e3dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342555} {:answer/id #uuid "6e54b5a5-5e25-5c12-a450-5d9b1393593f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342556} {:answer/id #uuid "5086bfa6-3589-54df-96f2-d31a64efb773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54918d71-7b8c-52bf-ba39-c9e57a0e3dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342555} :answer-field/id #uuid "c327756f-014f-5e94-9e1c-2ef1f0a0dc03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2edeb67d-5fd1-5efb-89bb-3d7d3065db3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65000" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-5x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+8u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u-5{u}^{′}x-10u+8u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}-{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f77e44d1-3039-5336-bcd7-09cbda079679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342560} {:answer/id #uuid "9cedd250-7058-5f5b-9220-7ced5644bdd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342561} {:answer/id #uuid "7da7d3eb-f048-5fa8-9428-073f493d4f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342562} {:answer/id #uuid "44d0b47c-ded1-5cc1-8acd-d6cff8a57cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342563} {:answer/id #uuid "093d9dc3-13ce-529b-bfa8-af8c0aff9e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f77e44d1-3039-5336-bcd7-09cbda079679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342560} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3afe736-189b-58bb-9149-0df0f8b3160e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fedd217-0218-5f91-9c96-64ee01c0e101" :question/math-academy-id "q-90510" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then what equation must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
